+**5.3 Composition and root allocation.** On B=eta=0, if additionally `P in k(L)`, then P is a polynomial in L: a finite pole of a reduced rational expression in L would pull back to a pole under the nonconstant polynomial L. Degree comparison then gives `P=f(L)` with f quadratic. Equation (UF) implies
+
+```text
+theta L = [3f(L)^2-(3/2)L(L+b)f(L)+(3/16)L^3(L+2b)]
+          /[2f(L)f'(L)] = h(L).
+```
+
+Reduce this rational function before examining poles. The same pole argument makes h a polynomial. Since `deg theta L=m`, h is affine with slope m; evaluation at zero gives `h(-b)=0`. Thus `theta L=m(L+b)`, so L has the one-monomial shape. The preceding coefficient proof (also valid at m=2 when B=eta=0) gives C_3. This is a conditional classification; membership `P in k(L)` has not been proved for an arbitrary solution.
+
+The obstruction to a quick divisibility substitute is explicit. On B=eta=0,
+
+```text
+P H=(3/16)L^3(L+2b).
+```
+
+At a nonzero root of L of multiplicity s, the possible multiplicities of P are **0, s+1, 2s**. At a root of L+2b of multiplicity t, they are **0, 1, t**; coinciding choices count once. For a common P,L root, comparison of orders `2r-1,r+s,3s` gives r=s+1 or 2s. For a common P,L+2b root, comparison of `2r-1,r,t` gives r=1 or t. The zero alternatives mean the whole root is carried by H. In particular, even squarefree L does not imply `L^2 divides P`. If one additionally knew `gcd(P,L+2b)=1`, then all P roots would lie on L with multiplicity at most twice that in L, giving `P divides L^2`; equal degrees would force C_3. The general equation does not supply that gcd hypothesis.
